# 🌿 PART 2: FENWICK TREE (BIT)

---

# 9. Core Idea ⭐

```text id="st10"
Store prefix sums in a compressed tree structure
using bit operations
```

---

# 10. BIT Structure

```cpp id="st11"
int bit[N];
```

---

# 11. Update Operation ⭐

```cpp id="st12"
void update(int i, int val){

    while(i < N){
        bit[i] += val;
        i += (i & -i);
    }
}
```

---

# 12. Prefix Query ⭐

```cpp id="st13"
int sum(int i){

    int res = 0;

    while(i > 0){
        res += bit[i];
        i -= (i & -i);
    }

    return res;
}
```

---

# 13. Range Query

```text id="st14"
sum(l, r) = sum(r) - sum(l-1)
```

---

# 14. Complexity

```text id="st15"
Update  → O(log n)
Query   → O(log n)
Space   → O(n)
```

---

# 15. BIT vs Segment Tree Usage Rule ⭐

```text id="st16"
Need simplicity + prefix sum?
→ BIT

Need flexibility (min/max/range updates)?
→ Segment Tree
```

---

# 16. BIT Trick Insight ⭐⭐⭐

```text id="st17"
i & -i → lowest set bit
controls range size in BIT
```

---

# 17. Problem → Pattern Mapping

| Problem                 | Structure          |
| ----------------------- | ------------------ |
| Range Sum Query         | BIT / Segment Tree |
| Point Update + Query    | Both               |
| Range Min/Max           | Segment Tree       |
| Frequency count queries | BIT                |
| Inversion Count         | BIT                |
| Order statistics        | Segment Tree       |

